{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md; cat paper.tex.prov.json; cat figure_manifest.json; cat writing_validation.json; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; ls .codex/skills/write-paper/scripts; cat paper_strategy.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md; cat paper.tex.prov.json; cat figure_manifest.json; cat writing_validation.json; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; ls .codex/skills/write-paper/scripts; cat paper_strategy.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: generate-concept-figure\ndescription: Generate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for paper generation as an editable draw.io diagram via the pinned Autoillustrate npm release, with a deterministic layout and an optional image-model restyle.\n---\n\n# Who runs this (important)\nDuring **paper generation the orchestrator owns this pipeline**: `src/scripts/paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` run the installed `autoillustrate` CLI synchronously as a post-draft step (`run_concept_figure_step`), derive every argument from `paper_strategy.json`, forward the selected outer agent, and promote the result to `./figures/<figure_id>.png`. The phase agents always run in a dedicated one-shot Docker container, even when the outer paper workflow is native; this limits them to the paper directory, selected-agent auth, and selected provider credentials. The paper-writing agent must **not** invoke this pipeline itself. The rest of this document describes the same CLI for standalone/manual use.\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for the paper when a high-level glance-value visual will clarify the method, comparison story, or evidence structure. The figure is produced by the Autoillustrate pipeline (the `autoillustrate` npm release pinned in the agent image): each panel is a collision-checked, deterministically laid-out draw.io diagram, the panels are composed into a single editable `.drawio` file, and the composite is optionally restyled into a camera-ready PNG. The deliverable is a real `.drawio` source plus its export — only the final restyle pass touches a generative model.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper generation.\n- The paper needs an early conceptual figure that is easier to scan than a dense quantitative plot.\n- The grounded artifacts support a clean overview figure without inventing unsupported mechanisms or claims.\n- `paper_strategy.json` requests a required overview-style `diagram` figure or the paper prompt calls for an overview figure on page 1 or 2.\n- The figure is a methods/architecture/training-loop schematic with 1–3 structurally distinct panels. (For pure quantitative plots use matplotlib/seaborn instead.)\n\n# Prerequisites\nThe pipeline shells out to several tools. Confirm them before claiming a hard failure:\n- **`drawio` desktop CLI** on `PATH` (headless export). Without it Phases 2/4 cannot export.\n- **`autoillustrate` and `headless`** on `PATH`, plus the selected agent CLI (`claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `gemini`, `opencode`, or `pi`).\n- For environment-authenticated OpenCode phase agents, set `PAPER_CONCEPT_FIGURE_AGENT_PROVIDER` to exactly one of `openai`, `gemini`, or `anthropic`; only that provider credential is forwarded.\n- **`OPENAI_API_KEY`** — the default VLM backend (`gpt-5.5`) for the Phase 3 per-panel inspection and the Phase 6 verification, and the image model (`gpt-image-2`) for the Phase 5 restyle. Without it those VLM calls and the restyle cannot run; you can still ship the deterministic Phase 4 export with `--skip-enhance`.\n- **`GEMINI_API_KEY`** — optional fallback. When OpenAI is unavailable, the paper orchestrator selects `nanobanana-pro`, `gemini-3.1-pro`, and `gemini-single` for enhancement and audits.\n- **A Python venv** with the toolkit deps. The container image bakes one and exposes its interpreter as `$VENV_PY`.\n\nBefore deciding a key is missing, check the active environment directly. OpenAI is the default; Gemini is the automatic fallback:\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os\nfor k in (\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"GEMINI_API_KEY\"):\n    print(f\"{k}: {'available' if os.getenv(k) else 'missing'}\")\nPY\n```\nDo not claim a key is unavailable when the rendered paper prompt says it is expected to be available unless your direct check still shows it missing. Without an enhancement key, `--skip-enhance` still ships the deterministic Phase 4 export.\n\n# What to do\n1. Infer the central mechanism, comparison, or evidence story from grounded artifacts only. Decide whether one cohesive panel suffices or the content naturally splits into 2–3 structurally independent panels (e.g. *architecture · update rule · training loop*). Prefer one overview figure, not a full illustration suite.\n\n2. Confirm the installed CLI and selected agent are available:\n```bash\ncommand -v autoillustrate\nautoillustrate doctor --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\"\n```\n\n3. In the project container, use the baked `$VENV_PY`. Native installations may let Autoillustrate create its cached venv or set `VENV_PY` explicitly.\n\n4. Run the pipeline against the manuscript. Resolve the strategy's exact figure id, pass `--output ./figures/<figure_id>.png`, forward the selected `--agent`, and provide a concise grounded prompt. Add `--skip-enhance` when no enhancement backend is available.\n\n**This pipeline can take ~1h** (it dispatches several selected-agent jobs and may run a VLM restyle). Run it **synchronously and block on it** — the caller must own the pipeline's full lifetime. Do **not** launch it as a background task that outlives the caller's turn (`run_in_background: true`, a trailing `&`, or a fire-and-forget agent step): when that turn ends the pipeline is orphaned and reaped mid-run, leaving the figure unrendered. In `paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` this is exactly what `run_concept_figure_step` does — a single synchronous invocation in the agent container, wrapped with `timeout` or Homebrew's `gtimeout` so a genuine hang is bounded. Native systems without either command skip this non-gating render step instead of launching an unbounded process. The pipeline writes `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (and `<figure_id>.prompts.json`) only on success; confirm those files exist before treating the figure as done.\n```bash\nautoillustrate ./paper.tex \\\n  --output ./figures/<figure_id>.png \\\n  --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\" \\\n  --prompt \"Methods diagram that will be the main figure of this scientific research paper. <one or two sentences grounding the panels in the actual method>\"\n```\nThe orchestrator creates `runs/<YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS>/` in the working directory and writes the deliverables to `runs/<ts>/figures/`:\n- `full_figure.drawio` — editable draw.io source\n- `full_figure.png` — deterministic export (no model involved; always safe)\n- `full_figure_enhanced.png` — publication-grade restyle (only when Phase 5 ran)\n- `figure.json` — the validated 1–3 panel plan that drove the figure\n\nWith `--output` set, the pipeline also copies the best available figure (the enhanced export when Phase 5 ran, otherwise the deterministic export) to `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and drops the plan beside it as `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`. The pipeline does not emit a PDF; the editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir.\n\n5. Keep the visual style scientific and simple: clean diagrammatic composition, exact labels, no decorative clip-art, no unsupported architecture details, datasets, or numeric claims. When the paper contains multiple figures, keep colors logically consistent — reuse the same color for the same method/baseline/condition across figures.\n\n6. Confirm the promoted deliverable. With `--output` (step 4) the pipeline already wrote `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` — verify both exist. If you ran without `--output`, promote manually from the run workdir (enhanced export preferred, deterministic export as fallback):\n```bash\nRUN_FIGS=\"$(ls -td runs/*/figures | head -1)\"\nSRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure_enhanced.png\"; [[ -f \"$SRC_PNG\" ]] || SRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure.png\"\ncp \"$SRC_PNG\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.png\"\ncp \"$RUN_FIGS/figure.json\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json\"   # the prompt/plan log\n```\nThe editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir so the figure can be re-edited later. The pipeline produces no PDF; the manifest does not require one for this figure.\n\n7. When the strategy or prompt asks for a required overview/`diagram` figure, do not silently replace this workflow with a hand-drawn or purely matplotlib/TikZ schematic. Use this skill first unless it is impossible.\n\n8. If a prerequisite is missing (for example, no `drawio` CLI) or the pipeline fails, log an explicit skip/failure reason in both `./memory.md` and `./global_memory/memory.md` before falling back to another figure implementation. Apply the same fallback logging rule if the generated figure is too poor to use.\n\n9. If you fall back to a non-drawio overview figure, record that fallback reason in the matching `figure_manifest.json` note so the run leaves a clear breadcrumb.\n\n10. If the final figure is used in `paper.tex`, add a matching `figure_manifest.json` entry that records `generator: generate-concept-figure`, `prompt_log_path` (the copied `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`), and any `fallback_reason`. If the figure is not used, do not force it into the manuscript.\n\n# Outputs\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (promoted from `full_figure_enhanced.png`, or `full_figure.png` when enhancement was skipped)\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` (the run's `figure.json` plan, used as `prompt_log_path`)\n- Editable source preserved at `runs/<ts>/figures/full_figure.drawio`\n- Legacy explicit-path form remains valid: `./figures/concept_figure.png` and `./figures/concept_figure.prompts.json`\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\n    \"path\": \"paper.tex\",\n    \"kind\": \"manuscript\",\n    \"sha256\": \"57c472a145c18b20f0f037569791dd281dfa8548eb1972ff67b12106c1fc2455\"\n  },\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[paper_data.json]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[analysis_summary.json]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[references_provenance.json]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[source_evidence/MANIFEST.json]]\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"Manuscript authored by the writing assistant from the listed artifacts; build_claims.py records its provenance but does not generate the prose.\"\n}{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"attempted_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"fallback_reason\": \"generate-concept-figure exited non-zero (rc=1)\",\n      \"figure_id\": \"fixed_game_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": null,\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/fixed_game_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/fixed_game_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"final_reference_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 4,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/final_reference_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/final_reference_comparison.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.reference_fidelity\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        4,\n        4\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"component_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/component_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/component_comparison.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.top_program_01\",\n        \"result.top_program_02\",\n        \"result.top_program_03\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        0,\n        0\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"reference_diagnostics\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/reference_diagnostics.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/reference_diagnostics.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready_for_synchronous_concept_render_and_parent_validation\",\n  \"all_checks_complete\": false,\n  \"completed\": {\n    \"latex_pdf_compilation\": \"succeeded using sanitized commands and canonical bibliography-target validation\",\n    \"section_and_citation_validation\": \"passed\",\n    \"claim_and_reference_provenance_validation\": \"passed\",\n    \"claims\": 143,\n    \"references\": 25,\n    \"checked_file_json_and_line_locators\": 91,\n    \"embedded_tables_match_regenerated_rows\": true,\n    \"evaluated_source_tree_identity\": {\n      \"matched\": true,\n      \"sha256\": \"sha256:91b3aef1fb3b5de21c8b8de8fb6a6dfa92d7809624ffe17f42c2c3af5b975082\"\n    },\n    \"copied_source_byte_identity\": true,\n    \"figure_manifest_schema\": \"passed\",\n    \"quantitative_figure_manifest_validation\": \"passed; sole full-manifest error is pending concept log\",\n    \"rendered_supplementary_information\": \"passed\",\n    \"visible_language_scan\": \"passed\",\n    \"page_budget\": {\n      \"body_pages\": 8,\n      \"reference_start_page\": 9,\n      \"supplement_start_page\": 11,\n      \"total_pages\": 15,\n      \"last_body_fill\": 0.8244503990548684,\n      \"minimum_fill\": 0.75,\n      \"method\": \"canonical geometric fill function with observed reference boundary and venue-brief budget; not character count\"\n    },\n    \"official_template_byte_identity\": true\n  },\n  \"pending\": [\n    \"Orchestrator must replace fixed_game_overview placeholder PNG and empty prompt log, then recompile and revalidate figures.\",\n    \"Canonical compile wrapper recorder rejects /usr/share/texmf/web2c/texmf.cnf, a system symlink resolving into /usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/web2c/texmf.cnf. Both are standard system roots; validator was not modified. Parent must perform final recorder validation in its environment.\",\n    \"Installed validator distribution omits venue config files; its automatic venue detection is unavailable. Canonical geometric measurement was called explicitly using the actual reference boundary and VENUE.md budget.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_report\": {\n    \"checked\": true,\n    \"required\": true,\n    \"figure_count\": 4,\n    \"referenced_paths\": [\n      \"figures/component_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"figures/component_comparison.png\",\n      \"figures/final_reference_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"figures/final_reference_comparison.png\",\n      \"figures/fixed_game_overview.png\",\n      \"figures/reference_diagnostics.pdf\",\n      \"figures/reference_diagnostics.png\"\n    ],\n    \"manifest_path\": \"/workspace/paper/figure_manifest.json\",\n    \"errors\": [\n      \"figures[0] invalid prompt log: Concept prompt log is an empty placeholder: figures/fixed_game_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"figures[0] PNG height too small: 680 < 700\",\n      \"figures[1] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\",\n      \"figures[2] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\",\n      \"figures[3] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\"\n    ],\n    \"hard_errors\": [\n      \"figures[0] invalid prompt log: Concept prompt log is an empty placeholder: figures/fixed_game_overview.prompts.json\"\n    ],\n    \"soft_warnings\": [\n      \"figures[0] PNG height too small: 680 < 700\",\n      \"figures[1] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\",\n      \"figures[2] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\",\n      \"figures[3] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\"\n    ]\n  }\n}compile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nvalidate_tex_refs.py\nls: cannot access '.codex/skills/write-paper/scripts': No such file or directory\n# Paper Strategy: Fixed-Game SHAP Reliability\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status: ready.** Use the `paper_standalone` family and `write-paper-standalone` skill for a thesis-first ICLR 2026 study. Provisional title: **Fixed-Game SHAP Reliability: Structure, Budgets, and Reference Limits**.\n\n**Central question.** For frozen tabular classifiers and a fixed empirical probability-scale Shapley game, how do sampled and structure-aware computations compare in single-call reference agreement and repeated-call consistency under finite coalition caps, and what reliability claims remain unsupported?\n\n**Central thesis.** On the evaluated German Credit and ACS income conditions, structure-aware forest attribution and probability-scale logistic integration yield perfectly consistent observed repeated-call top-three sets and signs and high reference agreement, but reference uncertainty, restricted game semantics, and unequal computational access prevent interpreting these results as general deployment reliability or equal-time superiority.\n\nThis is one shared scientific question with dataset/model-specific adaptations, not separate chronological experiments. The contribution is a bounded computational-reliability comparison and its interpretive limits, not a first-ever exact-tree algorithm, universal reliability theorem, or deployment certification.\n\n## Evidence and Qualification\n\nRead final evaluation independently of search and of any proposed downstream-study narrative. Here `paper_data.json.downstream_study` and `study.*` sources are absent. The final qualification sources report:\n\n| Grounding source | Reported value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `final.qualification.status` | `completed` |\n| `final.qualification.outcome` | `qualified` |\n| `final.qualification.correct` | `true` |\n\nNo failed final qualification is reported in this run. This outcome concerns the documented evaluation checks, not proof that explanations are valid reasons for high-risk decisions. Preserve the distinction in writing, review, audit, and revision. If later authoritative evidence reports failure, disclose it and retain its limitations; do not classify its cause as performance, validity, or adaptivity without evaluator diagnostics.\n\nMain numerical evidence comes from `final.evidence.episodes`. Final aggregates include reference agreement **0.9975639445869278**, reference top-three overlap **1.0**, and all-coordinate sign agreement **0.9997395833333332**, grounded in their corresponding `final.metric.*` sources. Candidate repeat-pair top-three and sign agreement are **1.0** throughout the retained final empirical observations. These are separate findings, not one undifferentiated reliability score. Controls, efficiency, failed-call fraction and replay are narrow checks.\n\nThe final wall total, **700.3707705293782 seconds**, covers the evaluation workload, including references and comparators; it is not per-explanation latency or a candidate-only runtime. The source brief's CPU-hour estimate is not measured usage.\n\n### What is actually evaluated\n\n- German Credit and **ACSIncome-2024-RELSHIPP**, each with logistic regression and random forests: four conditions under one thesis.\n- Final episode seeds: **104729, 130363, 155921**; coalition caps **64, 256, 1024**; **six** candidate calls per cap; **16** fixed query instances and **16** fixed training-background rows per condition.\n- German has **20** scalar feature groups and retained final splits **300/100/100** for training/validation/explanation. ACS has **10** groups and **20000/6667/6667**. Split counts are inspected resource provenance, not generalization measurements.\n- Preprocessing is training-fitted ordinal categorical encoding, numeric median imputation, and standardization of supplied original groups. This is not arbitrary raw-data masking or a one-hot-grouping experiment.\n- Logistic regression uses `lbfgs`, `max_iter=500`, `tol=1e-7`; forests have **64** trees, maximum depth **6**, and `n_jobs=1`.\n- ACS references enumerate the finite **10**-feature game. German references average two **4096**-coalition library estimates; their half-difference is a recorded disagreement proxy, not a confidence interval or exact ground truth.\n\nThese settings are traceable to the final episode arrays, `task/evaluate_final.py`, and `task/resources/final/`. The ACS relationship-field adapter and search/final predictor-content separation must remain explicit. Do not call the adapted task bit-identical historical ACSIncome.\n\n## Framing Choice and Alternatives\n\n**Story status: recentered.** There is no prior manuscript, edit-context file, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` directory. This decision narrows the broad source-task motivation rather than modifying an unseen prior paper.\n\n| Candidate story | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve the broad claim about reliable reasons in high-risk deployment | Reject: no human decision evidence, causal validation, conditional-game study, or background-shift evaluation. |\n| Shared fixed-game computation across model families | Select: both forest and logistic conditions have measured comparators and reference evidence, with inspectable component changes. |\n| Recenter solely on logistic quadrature | Reject as primary: discards useful forest evidence; no recorded branch coverage or total quadrature-error certificate supports making quadrature alone the whole claim. |\n| Center single-call confidence certificates | Reject: certificate decisions and acceptance coverage were not persisted; implementation presence is not measured utility. |\n| Narrate the selected search result | Reject: discovery order and selection scores do not answer the scientific question or establish final performance. |\n\nThe recoverable full implementation is under `results/ideas/03_probability_scale_logistic_shap_by_bounded_fourier_quadrature/program_1/src/`. The best-program manifest links this configuration to the final-tested method at selection/path level; the final program-tree digest is recorded but has not independently been matched to a recovered byte-identical final snapshot in this stage. Describe source-grounded mechanisms and evidence-grounded behavior separately.\n\nPrivate-only selection context must not leak into manuscript labels. Without `study.*`, do not manufacture a frozen-study selection contract, publish search score differences or attempt totals, or use the memory-only original-incumbent score as a comparison. The stored library comparators remain valid measured baselines.\n\n## Methods Worth Including\n\n1. **One-shot library Kernel SHAP — baseline.** Same model, empirical background, queries, identity link and nominal cap; `l1_reg=0.0`.\n2. **Two-start split-cap Kernel SHAP — baseline.** Average two independent half-cap estimates. Explain that endpoint overhead and actual prediction rows need not match one-shot cost.\n3. **Structure-aware fixed-game computation — proposed family.** Exact forest leaf-literal algebra and probability-scale logistic Fourier/Owen integration, with exhaustive nonforest dispatch when all subsets fit and the existing sampled fallback on numerical rejection. Explain the common estimand before model-specific computations.\n4. **Sampled path/regression configuration — development ablation.** Compared with the forest-enabled configuration on forest conditions; not the untouched original incumbent.\n5. **Forest-enabled, sampled-logistic configuration — development ablation.** Compared with the full family on logistic conditions; unchanged forest behavior checks component locality.\n6. **Same-game references and analytic controls — supporting evidence.** Exhaustive ACS, approximate German, zero/affine/permutation controls, and reset/replay. Do not count them as extra public datasets or as full nonlinear validation.\n\nInspection of `reliability.py` confirms that the forest comparison adds its import/dispatch and the logistic comparison adds its import/dispatch. Stored matched development episodes support direct component contrasts at seeds **1729/2718** and caps **64/256**. Label these comparisons development data throughout; they are neither final holdout ablations nor independent confirmations. Complete tables may go in the supplement, but the main component comparison must remain visible.\n\nDo not expand the paper into unmeasured neural models, background resampling, alternative certificates, leverage-score methods, external TreeSHAP implementations, or additional datasets. Relevant literature methods may be context, not measured baselines.\n\n## Results Organization and Statistical Meaning\n\n### Main final comparison\n\nCompare three methods at each cap in each dataset/model condition. Use normalized L1 **reference agreement**:\n\n`1 / (1 + ||phi - phi_ref||_1 / max(||phi_ref||_1, 0.001))`.\n\nShow individual episode markers rather than invented per-cap confidence bands. Preserve the distinction between approximate German and exhaustive ACS references. High-cap ACS one-shot computation can close the gap; split-cap averaging has particularly poor German agreement at cap 64. Report these negative and boundary results without an unsupported diagnosis such as regression rank failure.\n\n### Distinct reliability assessments\n\n- Reference top-three overlap uses absolute values and original-column tie breaking.\n- Reference sign agreement covers **all coordinates**, with absolute zero tolerance **1e-8**; it is not signed-top-three accuracy.\n- Repeated-call top-three and sign agreement compare candidate outputs on the same fixed game. Baseline outputs do not include six within-model repetitions, so a matching baseline repeatability comparison is unavailable.\n- Normalized efficiency residual uses a **0.001** denominator floor and is capped at one. It is not reference fidelity.\n\nExisting case-level 95% bootstrap intervals first average candidate agreement over repetitions and caps and then resample **16 instances**, using **1000** bootstrap draws and seed **84017**. They condition on a fitted model and its reference; they are not per-cap intervals and omit reference-estimation uncertainty. Do not average interval endpoints into a new interval. The same instances recur across episodes, and fitted forests may differ with seeds; neither repeated calls nor overlapping episode samples create independent people. Near-degenerate intervals from deterministic outputs do not imply negligible deployment uncertainty.\n\n### Limits required beside the main results\n\nResidual German differences cannot be assigned solely to reference noise or solely to candidate error. Deterministic outputs cannot establish the active internal branch. Nonforest exhaustive dispatch occurs at cap `>= 2^d - 2`; ACS logistic cap 1024 is therefore not evidence of the quadrature branch. Internal certificate acceptance/rejection, quadrature rejection frequency, candidate per-call runtime, and total discretization-error guarantees are unavailable.\n\nThe proposed family has model-parameter access; library methods use prediction queries. A nominal sampled-coalition cap does not equate arithmetic work, information access, or wall time. Do not claim equal-time superiority. The quadrature's analytic frequency-tail bound does not bound all discretization error; refinement agreement and efficiency are numerical diagnostics. Independent validation scripts are present but their preparation records say they were not run. Their presence cannot become a validation result.\n\nNo acceptable-error or user-decision reliability threshold was prespecified. State that one call meets the **observed same-game criteria in these retained conditions**, not that a universal trustworthy-explanation threshold was established.\n\n## Figure Specifications and Layout\n\nThe inspected ICLR template uses `article` and `paper,times`; `paper.sty` sets **5.5 inches text width**, **9 inches text height**, and a **single-column** layout. Full width means that one text column, not a two-column float. Half-width placement is not appropriate for the required composites.\n\n### Figure 1 — `fixed_game_overview`\n\n- **Required; diagram; generator `generate-concept-figure`.** Early placement on page 1 or 2.\n- One panel with at most six concise blocks: fixed game, sampled computation, structure-aware computation, repeat consistency, reference agreement, and scope boundary. Explain details in the caption rather than adding microtext.\n- Full **5.5-inch** width, approximately **1.6–1.8 inches** high plus caption; intended footprint about **0.23 page**. At least **9-point** final-size text.\n- Budget: **1 panel**, **1 per row**, **6 main categories/blocks**, **2 computational branches**. No invented numeric annotations or causal/deployment guarantees.\n- Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes` plus the inspected estimator source. This is an evidence map, not a claim that a confidence certificate was observed.\n- Use the orchestrator-owned editable draw.io concept-figure workflow later; do not run it during strategy planning. At most this one overview uses that generator.\n\n### Figure 2 — `final_reference_comparison`\n\n- **Required; grouped categorical dot plot; matplotlib.** Four dataset/model panels in a **2x2** arrangement.\n- Three cap categories and three method series per panel; per-episode points, one shared legend, explicit exhaustive/approximate reference labels. A common **0–1** agreement scale keeps the low split-cap outcomes visible; communicate near-ceiling differences through concise values or the companion table, not a hidden truncated scale.\n- Full **5.5-inch** width, approximately **3.5–4 inches** high plus caption; footprint about **0.48 page**. Roughly **2.6 inches** per panel supports short cap labels and at least **9-point** text.\n- Budget: **4 panels**, **2 per row**, **3 categories**, **3 series**. No search-order lines or per-cap error bars borrowed from budget-pooled intervals.\n- Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.reference_fidelity`.\n\n### Figure 3 — `component_comparison`\n\n- **Required; paired categorical dot comparison; matplotlib.** Two panels: forest component and logistic component.\n- Four dataset/cap categories per panel, two compared configurations, and the two matched development episode points. Show the relevant disabled/enabled contrast rather than all configurations in an ordered sequence.\n- Full **5.5-inch** width, approximately **2.2–2.5 inches** high plus caption; footprint about **0.32 page**. Short labels and at least **9-point** text are required; simplify instead of shrinking.\n- Budget: **2 panels**, **2 per row**, **4 categories**, **2 series**. Explicitly mark development data. No campaign progression, ranking, or final-set ablation claim.\n- Private grounding bridge: `result.top_program_03` identifies sampled-only, `result.top_program_02` forest-enabled, and `result.top_program_01` full computation. Use their actual `all_programs[*].evaluation_evidence.episodes` arrays and inspected source changes, not the scalar selection scores, as evidence for the plotted component contrasts.\n\n### Export and consistency contract\n\nQuantitative figures must retain source/data and export **PDF plus PNG**, PNG at **300 DPI**. LaTeX must use the PDF whenever available. For raster-only full-width assets, regenerate at **at least 2400 pixels wide**; column/partial-width raster assets require **at least 1200 pixels**. Never upscale an existing raster. The concept workflow does not itself promise a PDF: prefer one exported from its editable vector source when available, otherwise meet the raster minimum and preserve the prompt/source manifest.\n\nUse gray for one-shot library, orange for split-cap averaging, and blue for structure-aware computation. Component plots may use gray/blue for the explicitly labeled disabled/enabled contrast. Keep reference quality as text labels, not a remapping of method colors. No trajectory figures are warranted.\n\n## Body Budget and Reader Value\n\nTarget **8.5 body pages** under the supplied **8–9-page** brief. Figures and tables are already included in these allocations:\n\n| Material | Planned pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Introduction and early overview | 1.25 |\n| Related work | 0.65 |\n| Fixed-game estimand and metrics | 1.00 |\n| Model-specific computation | 1.40 |\n| Experimental and statistical protocol | 1.05 |\n| Results and component comparison | 2.00 |\n| Limitations and interpretation | 0.90 |\n| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n\nKeep two compact main tables: experimental geometry and condition-specific final reliability. The latter can present the existing conditional intervals at their true scope, alongside separate repeat consistency, reference top-three/sign agreement, and a nearby integrity-check paragraph. Do not force all diagnostics into an unreadable table.\n\nSpend available space on the common game definition, the nonlinear probability-scale mechanism, independent meaning of metrics, reference limitations, and fair-compute discussion. Do not pad with method history, oversized graphics, redundant tables, untested possibilities, or search scores. If ending at eight pages, its last page must be at least 75% filled; do not alter margins or shrink type to manipulate this. The supplied template's sample final-copy author block is not suitable for double-blind submission.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nFlush main floats, place references, then clear the page, enter appendix mode, and add **Supplementary Information**. The following substantive sections use available evidence; they do not require new experiments or guessed posthoc rankings:\n\n1. **Fixed-Game Definition and Preprocessing.** Expand the estimand, original-group representation, training-only preprocessing, exact data/split provenance and ACS adapter. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes` with inspected `task/evaluate_final.py` and final resource records.\n2. **Structure-Aware Computation and Numerical Safeguards.** Include leaf inclusion/exclusion coefficients, repeated splits and float semantics, Fourier/Owen integration, inclusion quadrature, frequency-tail versus discretization distinction, dispatch and fallback. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `result.top_program_01`, and the recoverable estimator files; label unrun validation scripts accurately.\n3. **Episode-Level Reliability and Reference Uncertainty.** Complete per-case/cap/episode tables, stored bootstrap intervals at their actual scope, reference half-differences, model diagnostics, controls and replay. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.metric.efficiency_error`, `final.metric.control_pass_rate`, `final.metric.replay_error`, `final.metric.failed_fraction`.\n4. **Component Comparison Details.** Full development comparison tables, source dispatch differences, unchanged-family checks, and recorded regressions/nonmonotone cap behavior. Sources: `result.top_program_01`, `result.top_program_02`, `result.top_program_03` followed to their episode arrays. No selection-score narrative or final-confirmation claim.\n5. **Reproducibility and Compute Accounting.** Dependencies, program specifications, seeds, offline data acquisition/provenance, hashes, commands, measured timing scopes, and exact figure/table script mapping once implemented. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.wall_seconds`, and the three final qualification sources, with inspected local specifications/resources. No identifying internal paths in the anonymous submission.\n\nAdd a separate **LLM Usage** section, preferably after these appendix subsections, describing documented ideation, code-preparation, framing and writing roles precisely. Do not invent model identifiers, human verification, or independent execution. Follow later authoritative appendix rankings without retroactively rewriting this strategy; never move essential final evidence or decisive limitations out of the body.\n\n## Literature, Access, and Disclosure Boundaries\n\nThe existing bibliography is a noisy retrieved candidate pool with metadata-level provenance. It is not evidence that every source was read. Start only with relevant permitted primary records such as `Lundberg2017AUA`, `Musco2024ProvablyAS`, and appropriate tree-attribution records after actual source verification. Do not use irrelevant retrievals as citation padding, invent missing Owen/empirical-Bernstein/semantic references, or claim unverified theoretical novelty. Nonessential certificate theory should not carry the central thesis.\n\nNo external paper pages were accessed in this strategy stage and no prohibited encounter was identified here. This does not certify earlier retrieval as reviewed or contamination-free. Do not attempt to identify the prohibited study or conduct broad rerun-reliability discovery. Stop and record any actual forbidden encounter, including where it occurred and how much was read.\n\n## Anti-Diary and Overclaim Guardrails\n\nAvoid headings **Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **First Valid Result**, **Iteration History**, **Experiment A / Experiment B**, **Winning Configuration**, and **Successive Improvements**.\n\nForbidden visible wording includes “we then tried,” “subsequent improvements,” “first valid result,” “next-best,” “later variants,” and the internal labels listed in the JSON contract. Also avoid “state of the art,” “certified reliable explanations,” “repeatability proves validity,” “exact German ground truth,” “equal-time superiority,” and “safe high-risk deployment.” Private-only source identifiers in this memo are not recommended manuscript terminology.\n\nNever turn unscored or unrun alternatives into measured losses. Never invent significance, certificate coverage, quadrature branch counts, candidate latency, robustness studies, additional datasets or external-baseline experiments. Retain the fixed small query set, fixed background, scalar encoding, approximate German reference, missing branch diagnostics, and absent human/causal/background-shift validation in the main limitations.\n\nThis stage produces strategy only: no `paper.tex`, no `claims_grounding.json`, no final-paper validation, and no claim of submission readiness or verified official-template provenance merely from inspecting the supplied local style.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
