{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,95p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,60p' concept_figure_run.log; cat figures/fixed_game_overview.prompts.json; sed -n '1,85p' REPRODUCIBILITY.md; sed -n '1,50p' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py; sed -n '1,45p' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json 2>/dev/null || true; command -v pdflatex; command -v pdfinfo\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '1,95p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,60p' concept_figure_run.log; cat figures/fixed_game_overview.prompts.json; sed -n '1,85p' REPRODUCIBILITY.md; sed -n '1,50p' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py; sed -n '1,45p' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json 2>/dev/null || true; command -v pdflatex; command -v pdfinfo\"","aggregated_output":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,graphicx,booktabs,subcaption,longtable}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Fixed-Game SHAP Reliability:\\\\Structure, Budgets, and Reference Limits}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\norm}[1]{\\left\\lVert #1\\right\\rVert_1}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nA feature attribution can be repeatable without accurately computing its intended explanation, and accurate without justifying a decision. We isolate the computational question for frozen tabular classifiers: agreement with a fixed empirical, probability-scale Shapley game under finite coalition caps. We compare library Kernel SHAP, averaging two split-cap estimates, and structure-aware computation using forest leaf constraints and logistic integration. German Credit and an explicitly adapted ACS income task provide logistic and forest conditions with different reference quality. Across the retained final evaluation, structure-aware outputs have mean normalized reference agreement of 0.99756 and identical observed attribution vectors across repeated calls; their top-three sets agree with every reference. However, German references are approximate, and the structure-aware methods access model parameters rather than spending the same prediction-only budget. Split-cap averaging is particularly weak at the smallest German cap. These results support a narrow computational-reliability claim for the evaluated fixed games, not equal-time superiority, robustness to background changes, or reliable reasons for high-stakes decisions.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/reference_fidelity]]\n% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/max_repeat_vector_difference]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nFeature attributions are often presented as a compact rationale for an individual prediction. The SHAP framework assigns additive feature contributions using a Shapley-value construction \\citep{Lundberg2017,Shapley1953}. Computing these contributions can require approximation, so an explanation obtained under a practical budget should be assessed as a numerical result, not accepted merely because the attribution interface returns a vector. This matters particularly when a small set of highly ranked features becomes the public-facing account of a credit or income prediction.\n\nThere are several distinct reliability questions. Does repeating the same calculation change the answer? Does the returned vector approximate the intended Shapley values? Is the underlying explanation game appropriate for the application? Does that game provide a justified reason for acting on the prediction? The first two are computational questions; they do not settle the latter two. Explanation disagreement across methods \\citep{Krishna2022}, dependence on background data \\citep{Yuan2022}, and the distinction between observational and interventional games \\citep{Chen2020} make this separation essential.\n\nWe study a deliberately restricted setting: a frozen classifier, fixed query, fixed finite background, and a probability output. The central question is whether one returned attribution vector is dependable as a computation of that \\emph{particular} game. Our hypothesis is that exploiting an exposed classifier's structure can remove substantial sampling error, but that repeatability alone remains insufficient evidence of attribution accuracy. The comparison is between computational access regimes, not just interchangeable random seeds: prediction-only estimators sample hybrid inputs, whereas the structure-aware implementation also uses tree arrays or logistic coefficients.\n\nThe study combines a held-out evaluation with matched development comparisons. The former supplies the principal measurements; the latter helps localize changes to the corresponding model family without presenting development selection as independent confirmation. Across the final German Credit and ACS conditions, observed repeated-call vectors are identical and normalized reference agreement is high. Crucially, exhaustive ACS references and approximate German references allow different strengths of interpretation. We retain this asymmetry rather than reporting every comparison as distance to ground truth.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nOur contributions are: (i) a fixed-game protocol that separately measures reference distance, top-feature membership, sign agreement, repeat consistency, and endpoint conservation; (ii) a concrete, inspectable comparison of sampled estimates with forest leaf-game and probability-scale logistic computations; and (iii) evidence about the limits of those measurements, including split-budget failure, approximate-reference disagreement, and unequal computational access. We do not claim a new general theory of explanation validity or a new invention of exact tree attribution. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the evidence structure.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fixed_game_overview.png}\n\\caption{Computational reliability for a fixed explanation game. Sampled and structure-aware calculations target the same finite-background probability game. Repeat consistency and reference agreement answer different questions; neither establishes deployment validity.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Attribution targets and computation.}\nShapley values originate in cooperative games \\citep{Shapley1953}; SHAP connects this allocation principle to additive prediction explanations \\citep{Lundberg2017}. Multilinear game extensions \\citep{Owen1972} motivate integrating expected marginal contributions over inclusion probabilities. Sampling-based Shapley calculation \\citep{Castro2009} and leverage-score sampling \\citep{Musco2024} address computational cost without requiring tree-specific structure. Exact tree attribution is established prior work \\citep{Lundberg2018}. Our contribution is the bounded empirical comparison and its interpretive limits, not priority for exact forest explanations. Leverage SHAP and a separate TreeSHAP implementation were not evaluated here.\n\n\\paragraph{Different meanings of stability.}\nPractitioner-facing disagreement between explanation methods \\citep{Krishna2022} differs from repeating a computation with the game fixed. Background-size effects in deep models \\citep{Yuan2022} also change a source of variation that we hold constant. Interventional and observational expectations can answer different questions \\citep{Chen2020}; accurate computation does not choose between them. Predictive multiplicity \\citep{Marx2020} concerns competing trained models, whereas our primary repeat comparison freezes each fitted model. Feature-selection stability \\citep{Nogueira2018} and rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010} provide context for comparing feature sets and rankings. Our reported top-three intersection is a simple membership measure, not either of those specialized statistics.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty and scope.}\nBounds for independent bounded observations \\citep{Hoeffding1963} and variance-sensitive empirical bounds \\citep{Maurer2009} motivate internal safeguards in the sampled fallback. Their presence does not establish observed acceptance coverage. Bootstrap resampling \\citep{Efron1979} supports conditional uncertainty over query instances, not an extrapolation over new populations. ACS-based tasks \\citep{Ding2021} provide a public income setting; German Credit supplies a smaller credit setting \\citep{GermanCredit}. Logistic regression \\citep{Hosmer2013} and random forests \\citep{Breiman2001} expose structures useful for this computational comparison. Tabular neural architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021} lie outside its measured model coverage.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Fixed-Game Target and Reliability Criteria}\nLet $f:\\mathbb{R}^d\\rightarrow[0,1]$ be a frozen classifier's positive-class probability, $x$ a query, and $Z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_B)$ a fixed background. A coalition $S\\subseteq D=\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ selects query coordinates and replaces all others with coordinates from one background row:\n\\begin{align}\n v_x(S)&=\\frac{1}{B}\\sum_{b=1}^B f(x_S,z_{b,D\\setminus S}), \\\\\n \\phi_j&=\\sum_{S\\subseteq D\\setminus\\{j\\}}\\frac{|S|!\\,(d-|S|-1)!}{d!}\n       \\big[v_x(S\\cup\\{j\\})-v_x(S)\\big]. \\label{eq:shapley}\n\\end{align}\nThis is an empirical replacement game, not a conditional-distribution estimator. Each supplied scalar-encoded original feature is a player. The probability scale and background remain identical across compared computations. In particular, logistic coefficients on the log-odds scale are not substituted for probability-scale attributions.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L11-L64]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L796]]\n\nFor an estimate $\\widehat\\phi$ and recorded reference $\\phi^{\\rm ref}$, we use normalized L1 reference agreement\n\\begin{equation}\n A(\\widehat\\phi,\\phi^{\\rm ref})=\n \\left(1+\\frac{\\norm{\\widehat\\phi-\\phi^{\\rm ref}}}\n {\\max(\\norm{\\phi^{\\rm ref}},10^{-3})}\\right)^{-1}. \\label{eq:agreement}\n\\end{equation}\nThe floor prevents division by a near-zero reference norm; the score is not a probability of explanation validity. Let $T(a)$ be the indices of the three largest $|a_j|$, with original coordinate order resolving ties. Reference top-three overlap is $|T(\\widehat\\phi)\\cap T(\\phi^{\\rm ref})|/\\min(3,d)$. Sign agreement is the fraction of coordinates with equal signs, treating magnitudes at most $10^{-8}$ as zero. We also report endpoint residual\n\\begin{equation}\n E=\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{|\\sum_j\\widehat\\phi_j-[f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)]|}\n {\\max(|f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)|,10^{-3})}\\right).\n\\end{equation}\nFor each pair of repeated calls, we compute the same top-three and sign agreements between their outputs rather than against a reference. These pairwise summaries are kept separate from reference agreement throughout the paper. Endpoint conservation is a consistency check, not proof of correct feature allocation.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1014-L1030]]\n\n\\subsection{Compared Computations}\n\\paragraph{Prediction-only baselines.}\nThe library baseline uses \\texttt{shap.KernelExplainer} with the identity link, no L1 selection penalty, and the requested coalition cap $C$. Its split-cap comparator averages independent estimates using $\\lfloor C/2\\rfloor$ samples each. Both use the same serialized probability predictor, background, and queries as the structure-aware implementation. This comparator evaluates budget splitting, not the effect of doubling total resources. Endpoints, background expansion, and setup costs mean a coalition cap is not a literal wall-time or total prediction-row equality.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L772]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L871]]\n\nrenderer executable=/usr/local/bin/autoillustrate\nrenderer prompt_flag=--prompt\n  █████╗ ██╗   ██╗████████╗ ██████╗     ██╗██╗     ██╗     ██╗   ██╗███████╗████████╗██████╗  █████╗ ████████╗███████╗\n ██╔══██╗██║   ██║╚══██╔══╝██╔═══██╗    ██║██║     ██║     ██║   ██║██╔════╝╚══██╔══╝██╔══██╗██╔══██╗╚══██╔══╝██╔════╝\n ███████║██║   ██║   ██║   ██║   ██║    ██║██║     ██║     ██║   ██║███████╗   ██║   ██████╔╝███████║   ██║   █████╗\n ██╔══██║██║   ██║   ██║   ██║   ██║    ██║██║     ██║     ██║   ██║╚════██║   ██║   ██╔══██╗██╔══██║   ██║   ██╔══╝\n ██║  ██║╚██████╔╝   ██║   ╚██████╔╝    ██║███████╗███████╗╚██████╔╝███████║   ██║   ██║  ██║██║  ██║   ██║   ███████╗\n ╚═╝  ╚═╝ ╚═════╝    ╚═╝    ╚═════╝     ╚═╝╚══════╝╚══════╝ ╚═════╝ ╚══════╝   ╚═╝   ╚═╝  ╚═╝╚═╝  ╚═╝   ╚═╝   ╚══════╝\n──────────────────┬──────────────────  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n                  │                                                           ▲\n                  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\npublication-grade academic figures from a methodology brief\n\nAutoillustrate · paper.tex · full mode\nAgent: codex / gpt-6.1-sol / high effort\nFigure: placement full-width · enhance skipped · audit disabled · unit tests disabled\nLoops: 0 panel inspects · 3 enhancement attempts · parallel panels\nRun dir: .autoillustrate/fixed_game_overview-20261107_033857-39192-32048\n\nPhase 1/6 · Plan\n  Planning the figure…\n  ✗ failed · 0s\nERROR: phase1 sub-agent exited non-zero — see /workspace/.autoillustrate/fixed_game_overview-20261107_033857-39192-32048/logs/phase1.log\n  log: /workspace/.autoillustrate/fixed_game_overview-20261107_033857-39192-32048/logs/phase1.log\n  ── last 50 lines of .autoillustrate/fixed_game_overview-20261107_033857-39192-32048/logs/phase1.log ──\n{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a1b440-846c-7c53-b861-88bedd8d9a76\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"error\",\"message\":\"Model metadata for `gpt-6.1-sol` not found. Defaulting to fallback metadata; this can degrade performance and cause issues.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"error\",\"message\":\"Missing environment variable: `RWE_CODEX_API_KEY`.\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.failed\",\"error\":{\"message\":\"Missing environment variable: `RWE_CODEX_API_KEY`.\"}}\n{\"usage\":{\"agent\":\"codex\",\"provider\":\"openai\",\"model\":\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\"inputTokens\":0,\"cacheReadTokens\":0,\"cacheWriteTokens\":0,\"outputTokens\":0,\"reasoningOutputTokens\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"usageStatus\":\"missing\",\"cost\":null,\"costBasis\":null,\"pricingSource\":null,\"pricingStatus\":\"missing\",\"billing\":{\"attempts\":[{\"route\":\"native\",\"usage\":{\"agent\":\"codex\",\"provider\":\"openai\",\"model\":\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\"inputTokens\":0,\"cacheReadTokens\":0,\"cacheWriteTokens\":0,\"outputTokens\":0,\"reasoningOutputTokens\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"usageStatus\":\"missing\",\"cost\":null,\"costBasis\":null,\"pricingSource\":null,\"pricingStatus\":\"missing\"}}]}}}\n\nPipeline finished · failed · 2s\n\nRun summary\nphase   time  tokens\n1 plan  0s    0.0k\n────────────────────\ntotal   2s    0.0k\nrenderer exit_code=1\n{\n  \"attempted_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n  \"reason\": \"generate-concept-figure exited non-zero (rc=1)\",\n  \"run_log_path\": \"concept_figure_run.log\",\n  \"status\": \"failed\"\n}\n# Reproducing the fixed-game SHAP study\n\n## Measurement and source files\n\n`paper_data.json` is the immutable measurement registry. Final qualification is independently completed/qualified/true; this is an execution/integrity/control outcome, not deployment validity. Main results use final episodes separately from development selection. No downstream-study object or additional task list exists. No new training, explanations, independent validation routines, or evaluator execution occurred during writing.\n\n- `source_evidence/selected/`: retained structure-aware estimator, dispatch, response interface, specification and unexecuted independent validation routines.\n- `source_evidence/sampled/` and `source_evidence/forest/`: matched development implementation snapshots.\n- `source_evidence/evaluation/`: final/development evaluators, acquisition code, pinned requirements and data provenance.\n- `task/resources/{search,final}/`: frozen records accessible through the supplied task link.\n- `analysis_summary.json`: descriptive reductions of recorded observations, not additional experiments.\n- `claims_grounding.json`: registry claim mappings and readable implementation evidence locations.\n- `source_evidence/MANIFEST.json`: hashes of copied evidence, for provenance rather than substantive implementation support.\n\n## Environment and public data\n\nThe supplied scientific environment has Python 3.12.15. Recorded requirements include NumPy 2.1.3, SciPy 1.15.3, scikit-learn 1.6.1, SHAP 0.48.0, pandas 2.2.3 and folktables 0.0.12. Their installed metadata was inspected. The complete pinned requirements are preserved, but a fresh installation was not tested:\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -m venv /tmp/shap-study-env\n/tmp/shap-study-env/bin/pip install -r source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt\n```\n\nThe evaluator requires Linux isolation, CPU affinity and Landlock/seccomp. A non-Linux port was not tested. BLAS thread limits are set in the evaluator; forest fitting uses one thread.\n\nExact acquisition URLs, digests, licensing, release metadata and normalization are in `source_evidence/evaluation/data_provenance.json`. German is UCI Statlog German Credit, DOI 10.24432/C5NC77, under CC BY 4.0. ACS uses actual 2024 one-year CA and NY PUMS releases, with native `RELSHIPP` replacing absent historical `RELP`, without an invented crosswalk. This is not unmodified historical ACSIncome. All cross-state predictor-content overlaps are removed before ACS selection; one representative per content group is retained with duplicate/conflicting-label provenance.\n\nUse the frozen resources for offline reproduction. To recapture independently, download every `sources[*].url` to `/tmp/public-source/<sources[*].file>`, verify the recorded SHA256, then run:\n\n```sh\npython source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py \\\n  --source_dir /tmp/public-source --output_root /tmp/new-resources\n```\n\nNo recapture was performed during writing. The frozen resources suffice for evaluation if upstream archives change.\n\n## Evaluation command (documented, not rerun)\n\n```sh\nTASK_SOURCE_DIR=\"$PWD/task\" /tmp/shap-study-env/bin/python \\\n  source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py \\\n  --program_path \"$PWD/source_evidence/selected/program.py\" \\\n  --results_dir /tmp/shap-final-reproduction\n```\n\nFinal seeds: 104729, 130363, 155921; caps: 64, 256, 1024; six calls per cap, sixteen fixed queries and background rows per condition. Development seeds: 1729, 2718; caps: 64, 256; eight queries and four calls. The full recorded final wall total is 700.3707705293782 seconds, including references, comparators, training and overhead. It is not guaranteed runtime, CPU-hours or candidate-only latency. No equal-time comparison is available.\n\n## Figures and tables\n\nRendering tools live in a private environment outside this manuscript workspace:\n\n```sh\npython -m venv /tmp/paper-tools\n/tmp/paper-tools/bin/pip install numpy matplotlib pillow jsonschema pypdf requests Unidecode\n/tmp/paper-tools/bin/python build_artifacts.py\n```\n\n- `final_reference_comparison`: final non-replay cells and baseline metrics by dataset/model/cap.\n- `component_comparison`: matched development cells for forest and logistic components, never relabeled as held-out evidence.\n- `reference_diagnostics`: final German candidate/reference distances and recorded reference half-differences, not error bounds.\n- All numerical tables: generated by `build_artifacts.py` under `tables/`. The manuscript embeds those rows directly to keep the supplementary section self-contained. Regeneration updates fragments, not the manuscript; a change of measurements requires an audited manuscript revision.\n- `fixed_game_overview`: orchestrator-owned conceptual render. The guarded placeholder and empty plan stub are replaced synchronously after this step. Reusable code cannot overwrite the completed render. It contains no measured-result placeholder.\n\nQuantitative plots export vector PDF and 300-DPI PNG; LaTeX uses PDFs. Full text width is 5.5 inches and plotted labels are at least nine points. Sidecars connect generated artifacts to recorded inputs. The paper reports pure reference sign agreement rather than misinterpreting the evaluator's mixed reference/repeat sign summary.\n\n## Manuscript and literature\n\nThe official ICLR 2026 archive was retrieved from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ICLR/Master-Template/master/iclr2026.zip`. Original style files are in `template_evidence/`, with copies at `paper.sty` and `paper.bst`; anonymous review mode is retained. `VENUE.md` fixes 2026 as the target year even though the repository contains other years.\n\n`curate_references.py` retrieves known permitted primary works by DOI or exact title through OpenAlex. Semantic Scholar was rate-limited. Primary records and available abstracts were read; citing/reference graphs and related pages were not traversed. `literature_evidence/` preserves the selected records. An incorrect initial Owen DOI returned unrelated tuberculosis metadata; it was rejected immediately, never cited, and corrected to *Multilinear Extensions of Games*. No prohibited study was identified or deliberately sought. Earlier retrieval noise was discarded rather than described as read literature. This does not retroactively certify every earlier-stage retrieval as reviewed.\n\n```sh\npdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\nbibtex paper\npdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\npdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\n```\n\n`writing_validation.json` distinguishes completed checks, environmental validator limitations and the pending diagram. The scientific-evidence report must contain zero dangling references. LLM roles are disclosed in the manuscript. Query-bootstrap intervals do not cover reference bias, retraining or background uncertainty. Structural access and small fixed query sets prevent deployment, causal, human or new-population claims.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate the exact TeX and BibTeX inputs observed during compilation.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nAGENT_SKILL_DIR_PREFIXES = (\n    (\".claude\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".codex\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".cursor\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".opencode\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".gemini\", \"skills\"),\n    (\".pi\", \"agent\", \"skills\"),\n)\nLATEX_SYSTEM_INPUT_ROOTS = tuple(\n    Path(path)\n    for path in (\n        \"/etc/texmf\",\n        \"/opt/texlive\",\n        \"/usr/local/share/miktex-texmf\",\n        \"/usr/local/share/texmf\",\n        \"/usr/local/texlive\",\n        \"/usr/share/texlive\",\n        \"/usr/share/texmf\",\n        \"/usr/share/texmf-dist\",\n        \"/var/lib/miktex-texmf\",\n        \"/var/lib/texmf\",\n        \"/nix/store\",\n    )\n)\nBIBTEX_AUX_DATA_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\bibdata\\s*\\{(?P<targets>[^{}]+)\\}\")\nBIBTEX_AUX_DATA_COMMAND_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\bibdata(?![A-Za-z@])\")\nBIBTEX_AUX_STYLE_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\bibstyle\\s*\\{(?P<target>[^{}]+)\\}\")\nBIBTEX_AUX_STYLE_COMMAND_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\bibstyle(?![A-Za-z@])\")\nBIBTEX_AUX_INPUT_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\@input\\s*\\{(?P<target>[^{}]+)\\}\")\nBIBTEX_AUX_INPUT_COMMAND_RE = re.compile(r\"\\\\@input(?![A-Za-z@])\")\nLATEX_ENV_ALLOWLIST = (\n    \"PATH\",\n    \"LANG\",\n    \"LC_ALL\",\n    \"LC_CTYPE\",\n    \"TZ\",\n    \"SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\",\n    \"FORCE_SOURCE_DATE\",\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate rendered Supplementary Information in a compiled paper PDF.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport re\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef _letter_spaced_word_pattern(word: str) -> str:\n    return r\"[ \\t]*\".join(re.escape(character) for character in word)\n\n\nSUPPLEMENT_HEADING_RE = re.compile(\n    rf\"(?mi)^[ \\t]*(?:(?:appendix[ \\t]+)?[A-Z0-9]+(?:\\.\\d+)*[.:]?[ \\t]+)?\"\n    rf\"{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('supplementary')}[ \\t]+\"\n    rf\"{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('information')}[ \\t]*$\",\n)\nREFERENCE_HEADING_RE = re.compile(\n    rf\"(?mi)^[ \\t]*(?:{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('references')}|\"\n    rf\"{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('bibliography')})[ \\t]*$\"\n)\n\n\ndef parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Validate rendered Supplementary Information in a PDF.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\"--pdf\", required=True)\n    return parser.parse_args()\n\n\ndef validate_rendered_supplementary_information(page_texts: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n    supplement_page_index = next(\n        (\n            index\n            for index, page_text in enumerate(page_texts)\n            if SUPPLEMENT_HEADING_RE.search(page_text)\n        ),\n        None,\n    )\n    if supplement_page_index is None:\n/usr/bin/pdflatex\n","exit_code":127,"status":"failed"}}
